" rtemp="0" transform="rotate(0 338 142)" style="fill: rgb(255, 0, 89); font-weight: bold; font-style: normal; font-size: 15px; font-family: bold;">out</text><g transform="translate(503,49)" x="503" y="49" title="codeHTML" width="497" height="621" class="codeHTML" name="codeHTML" rot="0"><foreignObject x="0" y="0" width="222" height="200" style="height: 621px; width: 828.333px;"><div name="htmlCode" style="zoom: 60%; height: 621px; width: 828.333px;" code="<div class=&quot;ch&quot;>
    
    <h1>Schmitt Trigger (Inverting Comparator with Hysteresis) - SPICE Time Domain Analysis</h1>

    <div class=&quot;ch-card&quot;>
        <h2>Circuit Components</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Op-Amp:</strong> LM741/NS (operational amplifier)</li>
            <li><strong>V3:</strong> Sinusoidal Source SIN(0 10 50) - 10V peak, 50 Hz</li>
            <li><strong>R1:</strong> 1 kΩ (input resistor)</li>
            <li><strong>R2:</strong> 1 kΩ (positive feedback resistor)</li>
            <li><strong>R3:</strong> 1 kΩ (reference resistor to ground)</li>
            <li><strong>V1:</strong> +10V (positive supply)</li>
            <li><strong>V2:</strong> -10V (negative supply)</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <h2>Threshold Voltages (Hysteresis)</h2>
    <div class=&quot;ch-formula&quot;>
        β = R3 / (R2 + R3) = 1k / (1k + 1k) = 0.5<br>
        Vth+ = β × Vout_high = 0.5 × (+10V) = <strong>+5V</strong><br>
        Vth- = β × Vout_low = 0.5 × (-10V) = <strong>-5V</strong><br>
        Hysteresis Width = Vth+ - Vth- = <strong>10V</strong>
    </div>

    <h2>Simulation Results</h2>
    <div class=&quot;ch-grid&quot;>

        <div class=&quot;ch-col blue&quot;>
            <h3>Time Domain Response (Top Graph)</h3>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>V(in) - Blue:</strong> Sinusoidal input ±10V at 50 Hz</li>
                <li><strong>V(out) - Red:</strong> Square wave output ±10V</li>
                <li>Output switches when input crosses ±5V thresholds</li>
                <li>Output is HIGH (+10V) when input < -5V</li>
                <li>Output is LOW (-10V) when input > +5V</li>
                <li>Inverting behavior with hysteresis</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class=&quot;ch-col red&quot;>
            <h3>Hysteresis Loop (Bottom Graph - V(out) vs V(in))</h3>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Rectangular shape:</strong> Characteristic of Schmitt trigger</li>
                <li><strong>Upper threshold:</strong> Switches at +5V (going up)</li>
                <li><strong>Lower threshold:</strong> Switches at -5V (going down)</li>
                <li><strong>Output levels:</strong> +10V and -10V (rail-to-rail)</li>
                <li><strong>Hysteresis width:</strong> 10V between switching points</li>
                <li>Prevents false triggering from noise</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

    </div>

    <div class=&quot;ch-card green-border&quot;>
        <h2>Conclusion</h2>
        <p style=&quot;line-height: 1.6; margin: 0;&quot;>This circuit is an <strong>Inverting Schmitt Trigger</strong> using an LM741 op-amp with positive feedback. The positive feedback through R2 creates two distinct threshold voltages (+5V and -5V), forming a hysteresis loop. When the input sine wave crosses +5V (rising), the output switches from +10V to -10V. When the input crosses -5V (falling), the output switches back from -10V to +10V. This hysteresis provides <strong>noise immunity</strong> - small fluctuations around the threshold won't cause multiple output transitions. The output is a clean square wave synchronized with the input frequency (50 Hz).</p>
    </div>

    <div class=&quot;ch-card&quot; style=&quot;background-color: #e8f8f5; border-left: 4px solid #1abc9c;&quot;>
        <h2 style=&quot;color: #16a085;&quot;>Schmitt Trigger Applications</h2>
        <ul style=&quot;line-height: 1.6; margin: 0;&quot;>
            <li><strong>Waveform Conversion:</strong> Converts sine/triangle waves to square waves</li>
            <li><strong>Noise Immunity:</strong> Hysteresis prevents false triggering from noisy signals</li>
            <li><strong>Signal Conditioning:</strong> Cleans up degraded digital signals</li>
            <li><strong>Oscillator Circuits:</strong> Used in relaxation oscillators</li>
            <li><strong>Debouncing:</strong> Eliminates switch bounce in digital circuits</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

</div>"><div class="ch">
    
    <h1>Schmitt Trigger (Inverting Comparator with Hysteresis) - SPICE Time Domain Analysis</h1>

    <div class="ch-card">
        <h2>Circuit Components</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Op-Amp:</strong> LM741/NS (operational amplifier)</li>
            <li><strong>V3:</strong> Sinusoidal Source SIN(0 10 50) - 10V peak, 50 Hz</li>
            <li><strong>R1:</strong> 1 kΩ (input resistor)</li>
            <li><strong>R2:</strong> 1 kΩ (positive feedback resistor)</li>
            <li><strong>R3:</strong> 1 kΩ (reference resistor to ground)</li>
            <li><strong>V1:</strong> +10V (positive supply)</li>
            <li><strong>V2:</strong> -10V (negative supply)</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <h2>Threshold Voltages (Hysteresis)</h2>
    <div class="ch-formula">
        β = R3 / (R2 + R3) = 1k / (1k + 1k) = 0.5<br>
        Vth+ = β × Vout_high = 0.5 × (+10V) = <strong>+5V</strong><br>
        Vth- = β × Vout_low = 0.5 × (-10V) = <strong>-5V</strong><br>
        Hysteresis Width = Vth+ - Vth- = <strong>10V</strong>
    </div>

    <h2>Simulation Results</h2>
    <div class="ch-grid">

        <div class="ch-col blue">
            <h3>Time Domain Response (Top Graph)</h3>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>V(in) - Blue:</strong> Sinusoidal input ±10V at 50 Hz</li>
                <li><strong>V(out) - Red:</strong> Square wave output ±10V</li>
                <li>Output switches when input crosses ±5V thresholds</li>
                <li>Output is HIGH (+10V) when input &lt; -5V</li>
                <li>Output is LOW (-10V) when input &gt; +5V</li>
                <li>Inverting behavior with hysteresis</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="ch-col red">
            <h3>Hysteresis Loop (Bottom Graph - V(out) vs V(in))</h3>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Rectangular shape:</strong> Characteristic of Schmitt trigger</li>
                <li><strong>Upper threshold:</strong> Switches at +5V (going up)</li>
                <li><strong>Lower threshold:</strong> Switches at -5V (going down)</li>
                <li><strong>Output levels:</strong> +10V and -10V (rail-to-rail)</li>
                <li><strong>Hysteresis width:</strong> 10V between switching points</li>
                <li>Prevents false triggering from noise</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

    </div>

    <div class="ch-card green-border">
        <h2>Conclusion</h2>
        <p style="line-height: 1.6; margin: 0;">This circuit is an <strong>Inverting Schmitt Trigger</strong> using an LM741 op-amp with positive feedback. The positive feedback through R2 creates two distinct threshold voltages (+5V and -5V), forming a hysteresis loop. When the input sine wave crosses +5V (rising), the output switches from +10V to -10V. When the input crosses -5V (falling), the output switches back from -10V to +10V. This hysteresis provides <strong>noise immunity</strong> - small fluctuations around the threshold won't cause multiple output transitions. The output is a clean square wave synchronized with the input frequency (50 Hz).</p>
    </div>

    <div class="ch-card" style="background-color: #e8f8f5; border-left: 4px solid #1abc9c;">
        <h2 style="color: #16a085;">Schmitt Trigger Applications</h2>
        <ul style="line-height: 1.6; margin: 0;">
            <li><strong>Waveform Conversion:</strong> Converts sine/triangle waves to square waves</li>
            <li><strong>Noise Immunity:</strong> Hysteresis prevents false triggering from noisy signals</li>
            <li><strong>Signal Conditioning:</strong> Cleans up degraded digital signals</li>
            <li><strong>Oscillator Circuits:</strong> Used in relaxation oscillators</li>
            <li><strong>Debouncing:</strong> Eliminates switch bounce in digital circuits</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

</div></div></foreignObject></g>